WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Google just built an ai design tool where you describe what you want instead of clicking and dragging. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners Episode 154. Artificial Intelligence or ai is starting to treat design the way a Chat already treats homework help. You know that feeling when you have a poster idea in your head, you open a design app, and then you just freeze, too many buttons, too many menus, too many chances to make it look messy. Google is testing a new

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:50.160
tool called Google Picks that tries to skip that whole mass. You type what you want in plain words, and the ai builds the design for you. No hunting for the crop tool. No guessing which menu hides the font settings. It is less like learning a complicated video game controller, and more like telling someone what you want on the screen.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:01:20.040
Picture asking, make a birthday poster with balloons and a cake, and watching colors, layout, and style fall into place. That is the idea. The friend doing the sketching is an ai that already knows how posters usually look. Traditional apps like Canva, taught people to click and drag. Google Picks is starting from prompts instead, which just means written instructions. Beginners get to skip the learning curve that used to stop so many projects before they even started.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:33.000
That matters if you are making a school presentation, a thumbnail for a video, or a sticker design for fun. It also matters if you have ideas but feel locked out by software that looks like it was built for professionals.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:46.400
Think of a giant salad bar with 200 little bins versus just describing the bowl you are craving. One path makes you assemble everything by hand. The other path lets you talk, then see a finished plate.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:59.720
Google is walking into a space long dominated by Canva and Adobe, but with that talk first approach. Over time, more people may try creative projects they would have skipped because the software felt too hard.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:12.460
For you, that could mean a social media graphic or a class project looking polished in minutes instead of a whole afternoon. You do not need expensive software and you do not need a design class to start experimenting.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:23.760
Google Picks itself is still being tested so you may not have the official button in front of you yet. You can still practice the same skill today with any free AI image generator.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:36.740
Open one and type a detailed description of a poster or graphic you actually need this week. Then add style words like in cartoon style or with bright colors and watch how the result shifts.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:45.440
The more specific your description, the better the output usually gets. Treat it like giving directions to a lost friend, not a one word riddle.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:58.560
While design tools are learning to take instructions, the apps on your phone are learning a different trick. Some work happens right on the device. Some work gets sent to huge computers far away, which people call the cloud.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:11.560
A new kind of AI chat app tries to pick the better path for each question on the spot. Easy stuff can stay on your phone. Harder stuff can travel out for extra brain power than come quietly back.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:24.580
Walking through a school day makes this click. You keep a small notebook in your backpack for quick facts. That notebook is like a local model, meaning a smaller AI that lives on the phone itself. Instant answers.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:36.080
Nothing has to leave your bag. Pretty private too, like whispering instead of announcing it in the hallway. Then a tough question shows up, the kind that needs a whole library, not a pocket notebook.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:52.520
The app sends that one out to powerful cloud servers, the way you would walk across campus for a bigger reference desk. You still get the answer. You often do not even notice the switch, like a car that uses battery around town and highway power only when the road gets steep.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:04:00.580
That mix is called a hybrid approach, which just means blending both worlds instead of forcing every request down one path.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:08.800
Fast when it can be, smarter when it needs to be, less information traveling over the internet when the easy stuff never leaves the phone.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:17.860
Perplexity, an AI answer app recently added support for a local model so people can feel that blend without a technical science project.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:25.700
The timing sits just before a major Apple event, which hints that companies want you to see you can choose where your data goes.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:33.820
Once you picture a traffic director, not one giant brain doing every job the same way, the whole idea sits still.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.980
And you can actually poke at that idea with your thumbs, which is a good moment to play.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:46.080
If you want the hands-on version, download the perplexity app and look for the option to enable local model mode.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:53.360
Ask at one simple question, like a homework definition you already know, and notice how quick and plain the reply feels.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.940
Then ask a tougher question that needs more detail, and compare the speed and the depth.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:06.160
The point is to feel the trade-off yourself, private and snappy on one side, heavier thinking on the other.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:14.000
Students who worry about privacy can keep some chats on the device, and still reach for the stronger answers when a topic gets dense.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.940
Now picture that same helpful brain, except it is sitting on a shelf in your living room, next to the playlists.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:29.000
Sonos just updated its speakers so they can pull in chat GPT, the chat bot a lot of people already type into on a phone.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:35.740
Your speaker can answer questions, tell stories, or play personalized audio without you unlocking a screen.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.040
It is the jump from a jukebox to a roommate who can talk, except the roommate lives in the speaker.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.880
If you have a Sonos speaker, go to the Sonos app, look for the latest update, and start there.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:53.400
Ask it to generate a short custom greeting, or a playlist description that matches your mood.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:58.760
Then try a bedtime story in a voice you choose, and walk around the room while it talks.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:06.900
Fully hands-free. Custom voices are a bit like picking a character's skin in a game. Only the skin is how your speaker sounds.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.340
The music setup you already use at night can feel like a companion, not just a black box that plays songs.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:20.180
Meanwhile, the everyday version of all this is already showing up in real life, not just in product demos.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.820
In New Zealand, people are sending chat GPT nearly 10 million messages a day.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:32.560
They are asking for help with budgeting, health questions, and hobby ideas. The way you might text a sibling.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:41.060
That is AI sliding from a fun experiment into a daily problem solver, like a calculator that also brainstorms weekend plans.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:48.360
Experts are also saying families should talk to kids about AI chatbots, because children are already chatting with them.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:56.520
The Australian National University has highlighted that need, as more young people use chatbots for schoolwork and personal questions.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:03.100
Think of it like teaching the difference between a calculator that can be wrong, and a textbook that still needs your brain.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.500
Starting those talks early helps kids use the tools thoughtfully, instead of tapping around with no map.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:15.580
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it.

